---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const strands = [
  {
    id: 'symbolic',
    kind: 'Artificial life',
    title: 'Symbolic reasoning in embodied agents',
    lead: 'Can a population evolve attention itself, with nobody telling it what "different" means?',
    media: '/research/oddworld-loop',
    body: [
      'Ten clusters sit on a ring, each holding five objects built from shape (four kinds), colour (four kinds) and size (two kinds). In every cluster exactly one object differs from the rest along one of those three attributes. Agents wander between clusters and have to approach and eat it — the clip\'s camera holds three clusters in frame at once.',
      'The brain reading each scene is a tiny generic attention network: one token per object, a few self-attention heads, a residual connection, a small scoring MLP — about a thousand numbers, with nothing in the architecture that knows what "odd one out" means. Those thousand numbers are the genome. There is no training step and no fitness function that scores an agent on accuracy: an agent only gains energy by actually eating the odd object, loses it by eating the wrong one, and lives, reproduces or starves on that energy alone. Whatever skill at the task exists has to pay for itself in survival, not get handed down by a reward signal that already knows the answer. A "neutral" control, run alongside, decouples the payoff from the agent\'s actual choice, to tell real selection apart from drift.',
      'The clip contrasts an early population against one two million steps later, same seed. Early on, agents aim close to chance. Later, they aim at the odd object reliably — and the attention weights show a mechanism: objects sharing the value of whichever attribute actually varies start attending to each other, a "who looks like me" pattern that only makes sense once the rule has been found.',
      'A direct descendant of the Polyworld lineage — embodied, foraging, selection-pressured populations — pointed at a symbolic/relational task instead of raw survival.',
    ],
    meta: [
      ['Substrate', 'Evolved multi-head self-attention (genome = the network\'s own weights)'],
      ['Task', 'Embodied odd-one-out foraging, implicit (energy-based) selection'],
      ['Status', 'Preprint, Zenodo, 2026'],
    ],
    links: [
      { label: 'Preprint', href: 'https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23291253' },
      { label: 'Code', href: 'https://github.com/drnamlabs-gif/oddworld' },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'collapse',
    kind: 'Cultural evolution and machine learning',
    title: "Rogers' paradox in language models",
    lead: 'If copying is cheaper than learning, who is left to learn?',
    body: [
      "Cultural evolution has a result that reads like a warning. Social learning is cheap: copy someone who already solved the problem and skip the cost of solving it yourself. So copying spreads. But Rogers' paradox is that a population full of copiers is no better off on average than one with no copying at all — copied information is only as good as the moment it was acquired, and in a world that keeps changing it goes stale. The benefit of copying depends on somebody still paying to learn directly.",
      "The same accounting now applies to machines. Language models trained on the output of other language models lose knowledge across generations, and they lose the rare things first — the tail goes before the common case does. That failure is usually discussed as a data-quality problem. Read through cultural evolution it is something more specific: model collapse is Rogers' paradox, with model weights in the place of a population and training corpora in the place of culture.",
      "A 2026 preprint makes that reading quantitative on small language models, and asks the question the paradox forces: who pays for the fresh data? Copying is the cheap move for any individual model, which is exactly why it scales — and exactly why the ecosystem cannot run on it alone.",
    ],
    meta: [
      ['Status', 'Preprint, Zenodo, 2026'],
      ['Substrate', 'Small language models, generational retraining'],
      ['Reads with', "Rogers' paradox · the Baldwin-effect work below"],
    ],
    links: [
      { label: 'Preprint', href: 'https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23298661' },
      { label: 'Code', href: 'https://github.com/drnamlabs-gif/rogers-model-collapse' },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'living',
    kind: 'Language-driven control of living systems',
    title: 'Prompt-to-Intervention',
    lead: 'Can you tell a cellular collective what to do, in ordinary English, and have it comply?',
    media: '/research/living-loop',
    body: [
      'Prompt-to-Intervention (P2I) is an architecture that maps a free-form sentence onto a bioelectric intervention on a collective of cells. A sentence embedding conditions a multi-head convolutional policy network, whose parameters are found by CMA-ES. The crucial move is the reward: rather than hand-specifying a target pattern, a vision–language model reads the resulting behaviour and scores how well it matches the prompt. Nothing about the target has to be written down in advance.',
      'The system generalises past its training prompts, which is the part that matters. Instructions it has never seen still land on the intended behaviour more often than not — evidence that what is being learned is a mapping between semantic space and dynamical space, not a lookup table.',
      'The current work takes this off the simulator. Xenobots — self-assembling constructs built from living cells — are the target, and the question is whether an ordinary English instruction can elicit a prescribed change in how one moves. The clip above is one such result: a biobot’s own archival trajectory, before and after being told, in plain English, to move faster, slower, or stop. Live assays are underway at Tufts. A patent application covering the general approach has been filed through the University of Vermont, and a manuscript describing the method is under review — so the mechanism itself stays undescribed here until that process is done.',
    ],
    meta: [
      ['Project', '“Giving Cells a Voice”, funded by the U.S. Army Research Office'],
      ['With', 'Josh Bongard’s lab (UVM), in collaboration with Michael Levin’s lab (Tufts). Douglas Blackiston (Tufts) carried out the xenobot experiments and made the video.'],
      ['Systems', 'ZapGPT · P2I · Xenobots'],
      ['Status', 'Patent application filed · manuscripts under review'],
    ],
    links: [
      { label: 'Code', href: 'https://github.com/namlehai90/ALife_ZapGPT' },
      { label: 'Video', href: 'https://youtu.be/u58QZ6P1RUo' },
      { label: 'Paper', href: 'https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.02766' },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'verify',
    kind: 'Simulated tissue engineering',
    title: 'Did the sentence do the work?',
    lead: 'A network can score well while ignoring its own prompt entirely — only a control tells the two apart.',
    media: '/research/p2i-verify-loop',
    body: [
      'Every tissue state on this page is a real run of BETSE (the BioElectric Tissue Simulation Engine), Pietak and Levin’s open-source bioelectricity simulator: ion-channel and gap-junction activity solved by finite volume methods, not an illustration of what a cell collective might do.',
      'A question runs underneath the P2I work above, and it is easy to skip past: when a system like this succeeds, did the sentence do the work? A network can score well while ignoring its input entirely. Six earlier single-cell controllers in this project did exactly that — each emitted a fixed intervention and never read its prompt — and nothing in a training curve gives it away, because a dead language channel looks like a model that is still learning.',
      'Three controls settle it, and they have to run beside the training rather than after it: retrain on shuffled sentence-to-target pairings, feed an empty prompt at test time, and hold back paraphrases that were never trained on. Run across twenty sentences and sixty simulations of a bioelectric tissue, the trained network does read the sentence, and the evidence is stronger than a drop in score. Handed a different target’s sentence it does not degrade — it builds the opposite voltage pattern, every time, on sentence pairs that differ by one phrase.',
      'The same three controls, run on an unrelated substrate — a single driven oscillator whose behaviour is known in closed form, so the best possible intervention can be written down rather than searched for — locate where the limit actually is, and it is not in the network. Sentence encoders place opposites close together: “go up” and “go down” sit at cosine 0.78, while “go up” and “head north”, which mean the same thing, sit at 0.32. The controller therefore picks up the axis and loses the sign. On the tissue the same bottleneck shows up a second way: the five phrasings of each request rank in exact inverse order of how much they resemble one another, worst where they share a template.',
      'Two independent measurements on two substrates, pointing at the same component. It makes the next move a measurement rather than a guess: more training will not recover a distinction the encoder never encoded.',
    ],
    gallery: [
      {
        src: '/research/p2i-verify-center',
        caption: '“Raise the voltage at the core”, target centre. Three rows, one colour scale: untrained weights (top), the trained network fed the wrong target’s sentence (middle), the trained network fed its own sentence (bottom). Only the bottom row lands on the ringed region.',
      },
      {
        src: '/research/p2i-verify-ring',
        caption: 'The matched control: “raise the voltage around the perimeter”, target ring, same three rows. Fed the centre sentence instead, the trained network does not default to the ring — it builds the centre pattern.',
      },
    ],
    meta: [
      ['Substrate', 'BETSE — Pietak & Levin (2016), doi:10.3389/fbioe.2016.00055'],
      ['Verification', '60 simulations, 20 sentences, three controls'],
      ['Status', 'Preprint on the controls in preparation'],
    ],
    links: [
      { label: 'BETSE (Pietak & Levin, 2016)', href: 'https://doi.org/10.3389/fbioe.2016.00055' },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'morph',
    kind: 'Evolutionary robotics',
    title: 'GPT-Morph: language to robot morphology that moves',
    lead: 'What if the sentence set both the behaviour and the body, and something checked that the robot did what it said?',
    media: '/research/morph-story',
    body: [
      'GPT-Morph is early work on turning a sentence into a robot design. A small language model reads the request and writes a spec: what the robot should do (forward, sideways, turning, airtime) and what its body must satisfy (number of legs, leg length, mass). One evolutionary search then finds the body and the gait together, in MuJoCo. The clip follows one run, from the request, to a random design that falls over, to the evolved walker.',
      'The closest work is Text2Robot (Ringel, Charlick, Liu, Xia and Chen, ICRA 2025). There the text goes through a text-to-3D model to give an initial shape; the body is then a quadruped, the behaviour is always walking, and the text plays no further part while the design is optimised. Here the text sets the behaviour and the body constraints for the whole search, and the result is checked against the sentence afterwards.',
      'The language model is Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct with a LoRA adapter, trained on 703 English sentences: templated requests plus paraphrases written by a larger local model for each combination of direction and body. Labels come from the class that was asked for, never from a model-written spec, and words that occur only in the test set were removed from training. On 23 sentences it had never seen, scored by rules fixed beforehand, the adapter got both body and direction right on 16. A 27B model prompted by hand got 11 — it understands the words but flips the sign of “right” in about half the cases — and sentence embeddings with a small network also got 11. The adapter handled negation and Vietnamese, which it never saw in training, and failed on unseen words such as “biped”.',
    ],
    figs: [{
      src: '/research/morph-pipeline.png', w: 1600, h: 1500,
      alt: 'Pipeline of GPT-Morph: a prompt, a language-model compiler, one 79-gene genome of 24 body genes and 55 gait genes, evolution of body and gait together, and verification by rules written before the run, shown with two evolved robots and their torso paths.',
      caption: 'The pipeline. The genome is 24 body genes (torso size, leg pairs, segments, lengths, joint axes) and 55 gait genes (a sine per joint, one shared frequency). There is no reinforcement learning: body and gait come from one evolutionary search. Below, two evolved robots with the spec each sentence compiled to.',
    }],
    after: [
      'The check is a set of pass rules written before the run and tested on held-out physics. It caught six ways the search was exploiting the simulator — joints moving faster than the servo allows, soft default contacts, falling forward counted as walking, one lunge counted as a gait, one jump and a fall scored as full airtime, and flickering foot contacts counted as flight — each fixed before the results here.',
      'Four robots from four sentences in three languages passed: six legs walking forward (1.70 m), four legs walking sideways to the right (2.02 m), a robot that spins on the spot (647°), and a long-legged hopper airborne 59% of the time. Nothing in the sentences mentions joints, yet sideways walking evolved joints on one axis, spinning on another, and hopping two long legs with the lightest torso.',
      'So far this has been tested in MuJoCo only, with one seed per sentence, so these are observations rather than conclusions. Transfer to another simulator and to a real robot has not been verified; that is future work.',
    ],
    gallery: [
      {
        src: '/research/morph-turn',
        caption: '“Un robot che gira su se stesso” — Italian for a robot that spins on itself. The compiler had only seen English. Four legs, joints on the vertical axis, 647° in 4.5 s.',
      },
      {
        src: '/research/morph-hop',
        caption: '“Un robot aux longues jambes qui saute” — French for a long-legged robot that hops. Two long legs and the lightest torso, off the ground 59% of the time.',
      },
    ],
    meta: [
      ['Substrate', 'MuJoCo; body and gait evolved together (79 genes)'],
      ['Language layer', 'Qwen2.5-1.5B with LoRA, 703 training sentences'],
      ['Related', 'Text2Robot, Chen lab (ICRA 2025)'],
      ['Status', 'Early work · tested in MuJoCo · code public'],
    ],
    links: [
      { label: 'Code', href: 'https://github.com/drnamlabs-gif/gpt-morph' },
      { label: 'Text2Robot (arXiv)', href: 'https://arxiv.org/abs/2406.19963' },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'embodied',
    kind: 'Embodied dynamical agents',
    title: 'Language-driven control of embodied agents',
    lead: 'Does the same trick work on something that has to move through a world?',
    body: [
      'Convince-not-control extends here from a bioelectric field acting on a cluster of cells to a driven Kuramoto oscillator whose coupling and forcing terms act on a Braitenberg-style vehicle. A reactive coach re-issues its instruction as the vehicle’s own state drifts, which is closer to how the cellular version actually has to work than a single open-loop prompt is.',
      'Skills compose zero-shot: a vehicle trained to move right and one trained to avoid a wall combine into one that does both, without retraining on the combination. Tested first on 2D maze and lava-crossing tasks, where a wrong correction ends the episode outright rather than merely costing some reward.',
      'This is the rehearsal track for a physical Braitenberg vehicle: the simulated substrate here stands in for one where the dynamics cannot be reset between attempts.',
    ],
    meta: [
      ['Substrate', 'Driven Kuramoto oscillator → Braitenberg vehicle'],
      ['Tasks', '2D maze · lava-crossing (MiniGrid-style)'],
      ['Status', 'Simulated; physical vehicle in progress'],
    ],
    links: [],
  },
  {
    id: 'eti',
    kind: 'Artificial life',
    title: 'Evolutionary transitions in individuality',
    lead: 'How does a population of individuals become one individual?',
    media: '/research/vitanova-loop',
    body: [
      'VitaNova is a simulation in which agents with evolving neural controllers self-organise into flocks — and then the flocks begin to reproduce as units, dividing to found daughter flocks. Role specialisation appears inside them without being selected for directly.',
      'The clip above is one recorded life cycle, not an illustration: a collective disperses, re-aggregates into a ring, then genuinely splits in two — the census caught this fission at step 2850 of the run (seed 7) — and the two daughter collectives are shaded separately from that point on.',
      'The point is not that the outcome is surprising; multicellularity and eusociality both happened. The point is that natural selection and self-organisation had to work together to produce it, and the model lets you take the two apart and watch what each contributes.',
    ],
    meta: [
      ['Published', 'Northeast Journal of Complex Systems, 2025'],
      ['With', 'Michael Levin, Richard A. Watson, Josh Bongard, Christopher L. Buckley'],
    ],
    links: [
      { label: 'Paper', href: 'https://orb.binghamton.edu/nejcs/vol7/iss2/8' },
      { label: 'Code', href: 'https://github.com/drnamlabs-gif/vitanova' },
      { label: 'Video', href: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0fqAUwj0fDk' },
      { label: 'Talk', href: 'https://youtu.be/Q-yZ-IeZywg' },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'learning',
    kind: 'Computational intelligence',
    title: 'Learning, evolution, and the Baldwin effect',
    lead: 'What does an individual’s ability to learn do to the population it belongs to?',
    body: [
      'My doctoral work concerned evolving self-taught neural networks: agents that generate their own training signal rather than receiving one from the environment. Across dynamic and rugged fitness landscapes, a consistent asymmetry appears — self-teaching buys robustness when the environment moves quickly, while social learning buys speed when it is comparatively stable.',
      'This is the Baldwin effect examined as an engineering question rather than a historical one: not whether it happened, but under what conditions learning is worth its cost to a population.',
      'The same accounting now applies to machines, and that question has become its own line of work — see Rogers\' paradox in language models, above.',
    ],
    meta: [
      ['Thesis', 'Learning and Evolution in Dynamic Environments (UCD, 2022)'],
      ['Venues', 'GECCO · PPSN · IEEE CEC · EvoStar · ALIFE'],
    ],
    links: [
      { label: 'Code', href: 'https://github.com/namlehai90/Evolving-Self-taght-Architectures' },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'markets',
    kind: 'Dynamical systems for markets',
    title: 'Phase transitions in price',
    lead: 'The same transition question, asked of a different collective.',
    body: [
      'A market reorganises without anyone deciding that it should — which makes it, formally, the same kind of object as the collectives above. I treat it as a dynamical system with identifiable regimes, so that the question stops being “is this reading high or low” and becomes “which regime is this, and is it building or breaking”.',
      'Working this way makes several timeframes resolvable as one object rather than several charts to reconcile, and makes the set of possible turning-point events enumerable instead of interpretive — including the transition that starts and then fails, which is the common case and the one most approaches discard as noise.',
      'This strand is commercial rather than academic: the method is proprietary and unpublished. It is built and running as Theta.',
    ],
    meta: [
      ['Status', 'Proprietary · unpublished'],
      ['Validated on', 'Equity indices, index futures, cryptocurrencies'],
      ['Shipped as', 'theta.bunnyquant.com'],
    ],
    links: [
      { label: 'Theta', href: 'https://theta.bunnyquant.com' },
      { label: 'More on the venture', href: '/bunnyquant/' },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Base
  current="Research"
  title="Research"
  description="Evolutionary computation, artificial life, language-driven control of living systems, and dynamical-systems analysis of markets."
>
  <section class="head">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h1>Research</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Nine strands, one question: how coherent behaviour arises in a collective that nobody is
        directing — and what it takes to steer it once it has.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  {strands.map((s) => (
    <section id={s.id}>
      <div class="wrap strand">
        <div class="strand__aside">
          <p class="strand__kind mono">{s.kind}</p>
          <dl class="meta">
            {s.meta.map(([k, v]) => (
              <div class="meta__row">
                <dt>{k}</dt>
                <dd>{v}</dd>
              </div>
            ))}
          </dl>
        </div>

        <div class="strand__main">
          <h2>{s.title}</h2>
          <p class="strand__lead">{s.lead}</p>
          {s.media && (
            <video class="strand__video" controls muted loop playsinline preload="metadata">
              <source src={`${s.media}.webm`} type="video/webm" />
              <source src={`${s.media}.mp4`} type="video/mp4" />
            </video>
          )}
          {s.body.map((para) => (<p class="wide">{para}</p>))}
          {s.figs && s.figs.map((f) => (
            <figure class="strand__fig">
              <a href={f.src} aria-label="Open the figure full size"><img class="strand__img" src={f.src} width={f.w} height={f.h} alt={f.alt} loading="lazy" /></a>
              <figcaption class="small">{f.caption}</figcaption>
            </figure>
          ))}
          {s.mediaAfter && (
            <figure class="strand__fig">
              <video class="strand__video" controls muted loop playsinline preload="metadata">
                <source src={`${s.mediaAfter.src}.webm`} type="video/webm" />
                <source src={`${s.mediaAfter.src}.mp4`} type="video/mp4" />
              </video>
              <figcaption class="small">{s.mediaAfter.caption}</figcaption>
            </figure>
          )}
          {s.after && s.after.map((para) => (<p class="wide">{para}</p>))}
          {s.gallery && (
            <div class="strand__gallery">
              {s.gallery.map((g) => (
                <figure class="strand__fig">
                  <video class="strand__video" controls muted loop playsinline preload="metadata">
                    <source src={`${g.src}.webm`} type="video/webm" />
                    <source src={`${g.src}.mp4`} type="video/mp4" />
                  </video>
                  <figcaption class="small">{g.caption}</figcaption>
                </figure>
              ))}
            </div>
          )}
          {s.links.length > 0 && (
            <p class="strand__links small">
              {s.links.map((l) => (<a href={l.href}>{l.label}</a>))}
            </p>
          )}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  ))}
</Base>

<style>
  .head { padding-block: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4rem) 2.5rem; }
  .head h1 { margin-bottom: 0.7rem; }

  .strand {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 15rem) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 2rem 3rem;
    align-items: start;
  }

  .strand__kind {
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.11em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--accent);
    margin: 0 0 1.1rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .meta { margin: 0; font-size: 0.85rem; }
  .meta__row { padding-block: 0.55rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--border); }
  .meta dt {
    font-size: 0.68rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    margin-bottom: 0.2rem;
  }
  .meta dd { margin: 0; color: var(--text-soft); line-height: 1.45; }

  .strand__main h2 { margin-bottom: 0.45rem; }
  .strand__lead {
    font-family: var(--serif);
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--accent);
    max-width: 38rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.15rem;
  }
  .strand__links { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 1.1rem; margin: 1.25rem 0 0; }

  .strand__fig {
    margin: 0 0 1.4rem;
  }

  .strand__gallery {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(16rem, 1fr));
    gap: 1.5rem 1.75rem;
    margin: 0 0 1.4rem;
  }
  .strand__gallery .strand__fig { margin: 0; }
  .strand__gallery .strand__video { max-width: none; }

  .strand__fig .strand__video {
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  .strand__fig figcaption {
    max-width: 30rem;
    color: var(--muted, #6a7179);
    line-height: 1.45;
  }

  .strand__img {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 44rem;
    height: auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: #fff;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  .strand__video {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 30rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--bg-raised);
    margin-bottom: 1.15rem;
  }

  @media (max-width: 52rem) {
    .strand { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 1.5rem; }
    .strand__aside { order: 2; }
  }
</style>
